<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Intef II</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. c. 2112–2063 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/21st-century-bce">21st century BCE</a> · Also known as Wahankh Intef II</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Intef II, also called Wahankh, was a king of the Eleventh Dynasty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> who ruled from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thebes">Thebes</a> for about fifty years during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-intermediate-period">First Intermediate Period</a>. He claimed the title of king of Upper and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-egypt">Lower Egypt</a>, fought the rival Herakleopolitan kings for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thinis">Thinis</a> and Abydos, and extended Theban control northward. His long reign prepared the reunification of Egypt by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mentuhotep-ii">Mentuhotep II</a>. He was buried in a rock-cut saff tomb at el-Tarif.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/intef-ii.jpg" alt="Intef II" width="320" height="481" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Stela_of_King_Intef_II_Wahankh_MET_13.182.3_detail.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Wahankh Intef II, also written Inyotef II or Antef II, was the third ruler of the Eleventh Dynasty. During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-intermediate-period">First Intermediate Period</a>, from about 2181 to about 2055 BCE, rule of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> was decentralised and split between several dynasties based in different cities, and his capital was Thebes. His parents were <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mentuhotep-i">Mentuhotep I</a> and Neferu I. His predecessor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/intef-i">Intef I</a> may have been his brother, and he was succeeded by his son Intef III.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>After the death of the nomarch Ankhtifi, Intef united all the southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nome">nomes</a> down to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-cataract">First Cataract</a>. He then clashed with his main rivals, the kings of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herakleopolis-magna">Herakleopolis Magna</a>, over Abydos. The city changed hands several times, but Intef II was eventually victorious and extended his rule north to the thirteenth nome. After these wars the rest of his reign was peaceful. A statue of Intef II in a sed festival robe, found at Elephantine, suggests that his authority reached the First Cataract and perhaps part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-nubia">Lower Nubia</a>. At his death he left a strong government in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thebes">Thebes</a> that controlled the whole of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-egypt">Upper Egypt</a>, with a border just south of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asyut">Asyut</a>.</p><p>He never held the full fivefold titulary of the Old Kingdom pharaohs. He did claim the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dual-kingship">dual kingship</a> nswt bity and the title son of Ra, which emphasises the divine nature of kingship, and on accession he added the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/horus">Horus</a> name Wahankh, enduring of life, to his birth name. The earliest dated mention of the god <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amun">Amun</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karnak">Karnak</a> is from his reign.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>The earliest surviving fragment of royal construction at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karnak">Karnak</a> is an octagonal column bearing his name, and he was the first ruler to build chapels for Satet and Khnum on the island of Elephantine. He started a tradition of royal building in the provincial temples of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-egypt">Upper Egypt</a> that lasted through the Middle Kingdom. His tomb at El-Tarif is a saff tomb, with a double row of columns in front of a large trapezoidal courtyard. In the reign of Ramses IX a royal commission, recorded in the Abbott Papyrus, found his pyramid-tomb intact. No remains of the pyramid have yet been found, and the tomb lies beneath the buildings of a village.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Intef II” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Intef II</b>, who reigned for about fifty years, fought the north for Thinis and Abydos and expanded the kingdom.</blockquote><a href="/state/theban-eleventh-dynasty">Theban Eleventh Dynasty</a></li></ul></section>
